    {
      "number": 778,
      "title": "lf wave: add the foreground progress runtime",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf wave goals              # loopflow owns the outer loop; Ctrl-C or wave/goals/STOP to stop\nlf goal goals -b --once    # one bounded inner pass, standalone\n```\n\n`lf wave <name>` runs a wave's outer loop in the foreground: each pass is a single bounded `lf -b goal <wave> --once`, and the next pass fires the instant the last one exits. It repeats until Ctrl-C or until `wave/<wave>/STOP` appears. A successful pass repeats immediately; a failed pass cools down 3s so a broken inner run can't hot-spin the loop.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoves Wave progress from model-owned looping into a loopflow-owned runtime. The model's own goal loop tends to get stuck; this makes loopflow the deterministic controller of the *outer* loop while `lf goal` stays the inner human/agent unit. This branch ships the **progress arm only** — the full four-arm design (pass launcher + monitor + cron + chat, two-tier memory) is folded into `wave/goals/MEMORY.md` for the follow-on work.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lf wave <name>` command (alias `lf loop`) in `lf/commands/loop.rs`: resolves a wave, loops bounded goal passes, inherits the terminal so the inner agent streams straight to the operator, and stops on a `STOP` file or signal.\n- `lf goal -b` gains a headless batch path that launches through the shared `launch_agent` runner instead of opening an interactive session; `prepare_goal_launch` now takes a `Surface` so batch runs go headless.\n- Setup failures (spawning the inner `lf`) surface as errors; only a pass that actually ran produces a pass outcome. Inner passes write their own durable logs, so the loop keeps no copy.\n- README documents running the outer loop in the terminal.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Monitor summarization, chat API, cron scheduling, and structured `<lf:pass-result>` parsing.\n- Asana roadmap reconciliation (the stored token was expired this run; tracked in `scratch/questions.md` and `wave/goals/MEMORY.md`).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 239,
      "deletions": 7,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 779,
      "title": "install: sync agent skills after local installs",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/install.py refresh --no-pull\nuv run python scripts/install.py local --use\n```\n\nBoth active install paths now run:\n\n```bash\nlf op sync-skills --global --yes\n```\n\nafter installing or promoting `lf`, so generated loopflow skills are refreshed under `~/.claude/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`.\n\nValidation run:\n\n```bash\nuv run ruff check scripts/install.py python/tests/test_install_script.py\nuv run pytest python/tests/test_install_script.py -v\nuv run pytest python/tests/\n```\n\n## Intent\n\nKeep local `lf` installs and vendor skill catalogs in lockstep. After a local install, Claude and Codex should see the same freshly installed loopflow steps without a separate manual `lf op sync-skills --global --yes`.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Global skill sync is desirable after an active install, but stale skills should not make the binary install fail.\n- `refresh` remains the fast CLI-only path; `local --use` remains the full promote path.\n- `lf op sync-skills --global --yes` is the source of truth for writing both Claude and Codex skills.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Run sync after installing or promoting binaries so the just-installed `lf` performs the generation.\n- Warn and continue on sync exit failures and launch errors. The binaries are already in place, and the user can rerun sync manually.\n- Cover the helper with installer tests using a fake executable to verify the real argv and the non-fatal failure contract.\n\n## Not included\n\n- No retry behavior for failed skill sync.\n- No app install changes in `refresh`.\n- No cleanup of stale wave roadmap mirror files.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 54,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 780,
      "title": "pm: close roadmap tasks with a PR link via lf op pm update --pr",
      "body": "## What\n\nAdds `--pr <url>` to `lf op pm update`. The flag posts the PR link as an Asana **comment** on the task and, combined with `--status done`, closes the task in a single call:\n\n```bash\nlf op pm update --id <task-id> --status done --pr <url>\n```\n\n`LOOPFLOW.md` (the wave's operating prompt) documents the flag so the Looping Agent uses it when a dispatched task's PR lands. The in-flight context the loop already sees carries each dispatch's `pr_url`, so the agent has the link to pass.\n\n## Why\n\nThe Goals roadmap item *\"Asana as the live roadmap\"* (`wave/goals/2-asana-roadmap.md`) is **Done when**:\n\n> A Goal completes a real Asana task end-to-end: reads it, does the work, moves it to Done with a PR link, with no local mirror in the path.\n\nEverything but the **PR link** was already shipped: the local mirror is gone (commit `c113ef04b`), the loop reads live via `lf op pm show`, dispatches work, and closes tasks with `--status done`. But the write-back could only mark done — there was no clean way to attach the shipped PR. `--notes` overwrites the task's description; the purpose-built `AsanaClient::comment` existed only inside `claim_item` and was never exposed to the loop.\n\nThis wires that existing seam through, closing the last clause of the Done-when. Kept minimal per the item's \"keep the surface minimal — don't build the full service\" guidance: one flag, reusing the existing `comment()` client method.\n\n## Maps to Done-when\n\n| Clause | Mechanism |\n| --- | --- |\n| reads it | `lf op pm show` → Asana `list_items` (already shipped) |\n| does the work | `lfq worker run` dispatch (already shipped) |\n| moves it to Done | `--status done` → `complete_item` (already shipped) |\n| **with a PR link** | **`--pr <url>` → `comment` (this PR)** |\n| no local mirror | mirror removed in `c113ef04b` (already shipped) |\n\n## Tests\n\n`cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy -p loopflow --lib -- -D warnings`, and `cargo test -p loopflow --lib` all pass. New/updated tests:\n- `apply_update_comments_pr_link_then_closes` — asserts the PR link posts to `/stories` and the task closes.\n- `pm_update_parses_create_and_close` — asserts `--pr` parses.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 165,
      "deletions": 39,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 781,
      "title": "Reintroduce wave ancestry so chords surface their children",
      "body": "## What\n\nThe Wave/Run/Session runtime reduction collapsed the durable model to three nouns but dropped the parent-wave field off `Wave` in the process, so `WaveAgentTree.child_waves` was hardcoded `Vec::new()` and a chord's contents (a wave whose members are waves) could never be observed.\n\n**Corrected premise:** the roadmap item and MEMORY.md claim \"the store already has the `parent_wave_id` column.\" It does not — migration `013_remove_chord_tree.sql` dropped `parent_wave_id`/`wave_type`/`position`, and `028_drop_chords_tables.sql` dropped the fallback tables. So this **adds a new migration (`044_wave_parent.sql`)** reintroducing the column, rather than surfacing an existing one.\n\n## Maps to the item's Done-when\n\n- **`Wave` exposes its parent relation; chord contents = children where `parent_wave_id = id`.** `Wave` now carries `parent_wave_id: Option<LfdId>` (with a `with_parent` builder). `Store::list_child_waves` runs `SELECT ... WHERE parent_wave_id = ? ORDER BY created_at ASC`, repos stitched. Covered by `sqlite_wave_ancestry_and_children`.\n- **`WaveAgentTree` returns child waves (not an empty list) for a chord.** `get_wave_agent_tree_handler` populates `child_waves` via `build_wave_dtos(list_child_waves(...))`. Covered by the new `get_wave_agent_tree_returns_child_waves_for_chord` test (parent + two leaf children in different repos → both surface); the existing leaf-wave test still asserts an empty tree.\n- **No \"member\" in the wave-structure code.** Vocabulary stays parent/child/sibling; the `migrations.rs` guard that the chord tables are absent is kept. Grep is clean.\n- **Two-repo chord runs with a child Looping Agent per repo.** The ancestry query supplies which children a chord would loop; the executor already launches one wave-agent Session per wave. The tree/store tests cover the two-repo chord *shape* (one leaf child per repo). The end-to-end \"loop the chord, assert one live `WaveAgent` session per repo\" path is not exercised by a new test here — see Follow-on.\n- **`cargo test` passes.**\n\n## Design choices (per `scratch/2-wave-ancestry.md`)\n\n- Chord-ness is **derived from having children** — no `wave_type` discriminator or `position` column revived; siblings order by `created_at`.\n- `parent_wave_id` crosses the lfd HTTP boundary, so it is mirrored on `WaveDto` (Rust), `Wave` (Python), and `Wave` (Swift) as an explicit `Option`/`str | None`/`String?` — **no `#[serde(default)]`, no `Default`, always emitted (no `skip_serializing_if`)** so the three mirrors stay in lockstep. `tests/fixtures/wave.json` pins a concrete parent value, asserted in the Rust, Python, and Swift fixture tests.\n\n## Tests run\n\n- `cargo fmt --all`, `cargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings` — clean.\n- `cargo test -p loopflow` — 833 lib + all integration suites pass (incl. `sqlite_wave_ancestry_and_children`, `get_wave_agent_tree_returns_child_waves_for_chord`, `wave_fixture_nests_repo_work`, `migrations::*`).\n- `uv run pytest` (contract/models/client/cli) — 95 passed.\n- `swift test --filter ContractTests` — 3 passed (`wave.json` parses `parentWaveId`); full `swift build` clean.\n\n## Follow-on\n\nThe Done-when clause \"a two-repo chord runs with a child Looping Agent per repo\" is covered here at the data/tree layer (the chord shape and the ancestry query that drives which children to loop). A full executor/integration test that loops a chord and asserts one live `WaveAgent` session per repo exceeds this unit PR and remains as a follow-on, alongside chord-creation wiring (nothing constructs chords with `with_parent` in production yet).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 250,
      "deletions": 12,
      "changed_files": 31
    },
    {
      "number": 782,
      "title": "Systems wave: north-star instruments, CI speedups, wave GOAL.md reshape",
      "body": "Early version of the Systems wave (renamed from `release`). Ships the measurement spine and a batch of sharp-edge fixes; the grind continues in follow-up PRs.\n\n## What's here\n- **Wave defs & prompts** — rename `release→systems`, `reduce→architecture`, `desktop→concerto`; every wave gets a loop-goal `GOAL.md` (identity-by-contrast, measurable metrics, an honest-question north, a stop discipline), formula documented in `PROMPT_STYLE`. Systems carries the billing/uptime/green-on-main/test-time north stars plus an **autonomy** metric.\n- **Measurement** — `measure_perf.py` (CI critical path + green-on-main), `measure_billing.py` (per-vendor spend/balance vs budget, keys via Doppler, never printed), and a daily `perf-metrics` workflow.\n- **CI speedups** — split `rust-lint`/`rust-test` so the two compiles run in parallel; cache `swift/.build` and Xcode DerivedData. Cuts the critical path (~2.7m bottleneck).\n- **Keychain fix** — dev app now signs with a stable identity instead of ad-hoc, so the `loopflow.connection.token` prompt stops recurring on every rebuild.\n- **Secrets policy** — `STYLE.md` now mandates Doppler and forbids printing secret values.\n- **Billing Level 1** — lfd was parsing streaming token usage and discarding it; now captured, persisted per run × wave × provider (migration 042), and aggregated. Answers \"wave X burned N Claude tokens, M Codex.\"\n\n## Follow-up PRs (the grind)\n1. Mercury-transactions-by-vendor — authoritative dollars-out incl. subscriptions, via `budget.json` `match` patterns\n2. Cloudflare account-id + Fly cost field (finish the two \"unavailable\" vendors)\n3. tmux-path token capture (Level 1 only sees the in-process executor today)\n4. Wire collectors into the daily workflow; drop the mislabeled `aws` vendor (it's Cloudflare R2)\n\n## Notes\n- Verified locally: `cargo fmt`/`clippy`/`test` green for the lfd changes; both collectors run against live data secret-free.\n- `budget.json`'s \"aws\" is a misnomer — those creds are Cloudflare R2 (region `auto`).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2406,
      "deletions": 197,
      "changed_files": 53
    },
    {
      "number": 785,
      "title": "ci: trim concerto-ui-test post-test hang (~45s/run)",
      "body": "The `concerto-ui-test` step spends ~43% of its wall-clock *idle*: the Concerto GUI app used as the xcodebuild test host reliably fails to exit on headless macos-15, so the run hangs after the suite passes. The harness already force-kills once success is proven (XCTest passed + ≥30 Swift Testing suites passed + no failures) — but waits a fixed 60s grace first. Those success signals land *before* the wait, so the grace can only bound the post-test hang, never truncate a live build or test.\n\nShrinking `idle_success_seconds` 60→15 saves **~45s/run** (~30% off the critical-path bottleneck). Additive to the DerivedData caching already on main — that trims the ~82s build, this trims the ~61s hang.\n\nVerified against real CI logs (a green run fired the 61s idle-kill). Can't repro locally — the hang only happens headless (locally the app has a display and exits normally). One-line value change + comment.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 6,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 787,
      "title": "ci: run rust tests with cargo-nextest (~15-30s/run)",
      "body": "The rust suite is ~1031 tests across ~20 test binaries that overwhelmingly shell out to `git`. `cargo test --all` runs the binaries serially (parallelizing only within each), so runners sit blocked-on-I/O between binaries. nextest overlaps them.\n\nLocal (warm build): `cargo test --all` ~40s → `cargo nextest run --all` ~12s across three runs each. Zero doctests in the crate, so nextest (which skips doctests) covers the whole suite. Conservative CI estimate: ~15-30s off `rust-test`, net of the prebuilt `taiki-e/install-action@nextest` step (~1-2s).\n\nSame tests, same semantics; 1031/1031 passed every local run. `docker-smoke`'s filtered `cargo test` left untouched to keep blast radius minimal.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 788,
      "title": "ci: -gnone on swift-test build (~10-15s/run)",
      "body": "`swift test` in this job is ~95% compilation, ~0% execution (281 tests run in 0.25s). `-Xswiftc -gnone` skips debug-info generation (DWARF per file + dSYM on links), cutting the clean build 33s→28s locally (~15%), roughly **10-15s** off the ~1.7min job. Unlike dep-trimming this lands on cold *and* warm-cache runs, orthogonal to #782's `.build` cache.\n\nRisk low: Swift Testing `#expect` and XCTest failure locations come from compile-time `#file/#line` macros, not debug info — verified a deliberate failing `#expect` under -gnone still reported `WaveTests.swift:830:9: Expectation failed`. Only trade-off: a rare native-crash backtrace would be unsymbolicated (fine for CI; one flag to re-enable for a debug run).\n\nFollow-up (not here): `swift test` links a 32MB standalone Concerto executable the tests never use (~5s) — killing it needs a library/executable module split, too risky for this PR.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 4,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 789,
      "title": "scripts/test.py: changed-aware test runner for the gate loop",
      "body": "The real dev-loop cost isn't CI wall-clock — it's re-running the *whole* test matrix on every `lf gate` after a rebase or bugfix. This adds a changed-aware runner so gate re-runs only the suites the branch actually touches.\n\n`scripts/test.py` (stdlib only): diffs vs the branch base, maps changed paths → the 7 CI suites, and runs only the selected ones with the fast runners (`cargo nextest`, `swift test -gnone`), fast-first, non-zero on failure.\n\n- Default = changed-only; `--all` = everything; `--list` = plan without running; `--base REF`; per-suite force flags.\n- Slow suites (concerto/e2e/docker) stay off in changed-mode and print why — run them with `--all` or their flag.\n- rust-only change → runs just `cargo nextest`; mixed change → the touched slice; python-test-only → scopes pytest to the changed files.\n\nWired in: `gate.md` tells the agent to use `scripts/test.py` for the iterative loop and `--all` before final ship; `TESTING.md` documents it (examples-first + the path→suite table). Same fast runners the CI PRs (#787 nextest, #788 -gnone) use — so CI and the local loop share one story.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 479,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 790,
      "title": "ci: make Concerto test host exit cleanly (delete the idle-kill hack)",
      "body": "Root-causes the concerto-ui-test post-test hang instead of trimming the grace. A `ConcertoTestsPrincipal` (`XCTestObservation`, registered as the test bundle's `NSPrincipalClass`) calls `NSApp.terminate` on `testBundleDidFinish`, so the hosted app quits and `xcodebuild test` exits on its own. Deletes the ~200-line Python idle-kill wrapper — the step is now a plain `xcodebuild test`.\n\nKey details:\n- `testBundleDidFinish` fires **after** all 281 tests (XCTest + Swift Testing) — verified the callback order locally, so it can't truncate the run.\n- `NSApp.terminate` (not `exit()`): `exit(0)` makes xcodebuild report TEST FAILED (runner died early); terminate drains the run loop for a clean exit 0. Local wall time ~60s→13s.\n- Supersedes #785's idle=15 band-aid (that branch kept as fallback).\n\n⚠️ **Needs the CI run to confirm** — the headless hang can't be reproduced locally (my Mac has a GUI). If `NSApp` were somehow nil on CI the job would hang to its 12-min timeout (bounded, loud). Watching this PR's concerto-ui-test before merge; not auto-merging.\n\nBigger follow-up the agent surfaced: `swift-test` already runs all 281 ConcertoTests un-hosted in 0.6s, so concerto-ui-test's test *execution* is redundant — its only unique signal is 'the app target compiles.' Converting it to `build-for-testing` (or dropping it) would remove the hosted run and the hang entirely. Separate decision.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 52,
      "deletions": 207,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 792,
      "title": "lfd: repo-grain token usage + `lf usage` command",
      "body": "The attribution primitive for cross-repo spend allocation. Extends token-usage tracking to **repo × provider** and exposes it.\n\n- Migration **046** adds a nullable `repo` to `run_token_usage` + a `(repo, provider)` index; `persist_run_usage` tags rows with `run.repo`.\n- `TokenUsageReport` gains `by_repo_provider` alongside `by_wave_provider`/`by_provider`; new `AggregateTokenUsageByRepoProvider` query.\n- `GET /v0/usage` returns a `UsageReportDto` (wire DTO: no serde defaults, every field required-or-Optional) + a round-trip fixture (`tests/fixtures/dto/usage_report.json`, includes a `repo: null` row).\n- `lf usage` prints repo × provider × tokens + per-provider rollup + grand total.\n\nThis is what the studio billing dashboard consumes: allocate each vendor's Mercury dollars across repos by token share. Rebased onto main (migration renumbered 043→046 after #782's 042–045); clippy + 841 tests green incl. the repo×provider aggregation round-trip and the DTO fixture.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 558,
      "deletions": 23,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 795,
      "title": "ci: restore green main — concerto build-for-testing + drop flaky bump test",
      "body": "#790's self-terminating test host is **intermittent** — it exited fast on the PR run but on the post-merge main run it didn't fire and hung ~11min to the step timeout (red main), and #790 had deleted the idle-kill safety net.\n\nDeterministic fix: the hosted test *run* is redundant (swift-test runs the same suites un-hosted, green). concerto-ui-test's only unique signal is that the Concerto app target compiles — `xcodebuild build-for-testing` gives exactly that without launching the app, so there's no test-host run loop to hang on. Robust by construction, not by luck. Faster too (no run).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 7,
      "deletions": 7,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 796,
      "title": "wave: reactive server replacing the goal loop — one persistent mind, chat/memory speech surface, harness engine",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf wave designer             # the wave's server: one persistent mind, until Ctrl-C\nlf chat \"ship the button audit first\"     # speak into its thread (any process can)\nlf memory add \"buttons: variants unified\" # curate what it knows\nlf wave s3                   # the s3 (control) VSM charter\nlf q worker run designer --flow build --task \"unify button variants\"\nbash scripts/demo_wave.sh    # the guided demo\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the grind-loop `lf goal` command with a long-lived reactive wave server. `lf wave <name>` starts one persistent codex thread in the wave's own worktree that handles both progress and chat as a single conversation: messages queue mid-turn, steer redirects a live turn, interrupt finalizes a partial one. Truth is an append-only journal, so a restart replays the whole thread. `lf chat` and `lf memory` are the new speech surface — the same doors for minds, workers, humans, and scripts; a publish with no subscriber drops silently (exit 0) so the verbs are safe in any prompt. Worker reports arrive attributed in the thread. On the client side, Concerto swaps its tmux terminal-workspace surface for a live WaveChat pane.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Wave runtime** (`lfd/wave/`): new `journal`, `mind`, `registry`, `runtime`, `server`, `state`, and `memory` modules — the event-log spine, the one-thread mind, and the store-as-registry model (no daemon required). README documents the wire contract.\n- **Conversations harness** (`lfd/conversations/harness/`): unified engine over claude, codex, and opencode vendors with per-vendor mapping, conformance tests, and ndjson/jsonl testdata; a live-codex smoke test targets the 0.142.5 app-server protocol.\n- **lf commands**: delete `goal` and the standalone `loop`; add `chat`, `memory`, and `q`; new `session.rs` and `wave_context.rs` give every `lf run` ambient wave context. `chat`/`memory` join `KNOWN_COMMANDS`.\n- **Worker placement**: `--pool` (shared wave worktree, shared branch) and `--stack <run-id>` (fork a dependent series off an unlanded branch); default stays a fresh sibling worktree. Threaded through the Python CLI, client, and API with DTO fixtures and mutual-exclusion validation.\n- **Concerto**: WaveChat view, message rendering, and connection tests replace the removed `TerminalWorkspaceView`; `WaveOrigin` handles bundled-local vs remote lfd.\n- **Dispatch/transport**: one tmux wrapper (no env poisoning, probed liveness at every tier), reqwest transport, SIGHUP/SIGTERM cleanup.\n- **Store**: migrations rename `runs`/`wave_run_id` and add `run_id` to terminal sessions; new DTO fixtures and wire types shed defaults.\n- **Waves data**: rename `reduce→architecture`, `release→systems`, `desktop→concerto`; Asana is the roadmap, so file-based roadmap items are wiped and `GOAL.md`/`MEMORY.md` reconciled.\n- Extensive design and research docs land under `scratch/` (wave-agent design, prior-art briefs, free-energy synthesis).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 23644,
      "deletions": 3172,
      "changed_files": 152
    },
    {
      "number": 797,
      "title": "lf runs + trace: the local run ledger, plus prompt trims",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf runs              # list recent runs from the local ledger\nlf trace <run_id>    # replay one run's events, tokens, and cost\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds a local run ledger. Streaming agents now feed token and cost events into the journal, which persists them to a new `run_events` sqlite table (migration 047). `lf runs` lists recent runs; `lf trace <run_id>` reads back a single run's events, token usage, and cost. Prompt logs gain a `{run_id}` filename segment (present when `LF_RUN_ID` is set) so a run's prompt log joins to its journal records. The journal now records in any repo, not just wave worktrees.\n\nAlongside the ledger, this branch trims the prompt system and tightens the design flow. Voice docs and the interactive-surface preambles (cli, concerto mac/iphone) are gone — only the headless surface still needs a preamble, since no user is present to speak for it. The design and kickoff steps now require every design to **name its demo**: the moment that proves the win, the command a developer runs and what they see, with an explicit infra-only exception.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `lf runs` / `lf trace` commands backed by a `run_events` table and journal recording of usage/cost\n- Prompt-log filenames carry the run id to join logs to the ledger\n- Removed voice doc resolution and cli/concerto surface preambles; only headless keeps one\n- Design + kickoff steps require naming the demo; `ship-wave`→`ship`, changed-aware test guidance made repo-agnostic\n- `lf pm` connects the meta and systems waves to Asana; new `wave/meta/GOAL.md` and updated `wave/systems/GOAL.md`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1161,
      "deletions": 295,
      "changed_files": 36
    },
    {
      "number": 801,
      "title": "wave realignment + the lfd/lfq/lfdb collapse",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf wave designer                          # start the wave's server: one persistent mind\nlf chat \"ship the button audit first\"     # post into its thread (any process can)\nlf memory add \"buttons: variants unified\" # curate what it knows\ntmux ls                                   # the wave agent and its workers are plain tmux\n```\n\n```bash\nlf sub ...          # new subprocess/worker dispatch surface\nlf op queue ...     # queue reconcile / next, replacing the trigger machinery\nlf q ...            # query lfd (replaces the old Python `lfq` tool)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis collapses the daemon's sprawling subsystems into a single reactive wave model and drops the layers that no longer earn their place. The wave runtime moves out of `lfd/` to a top-level `wave/` module built around a persistent resident mind, an append-only journal, and a message channel — progress and chat are one conversation, human messages steer a live turn while attributed messages (workers, scripts) queue. The store layer is lifted out of `lfd/store` into its own `lfdb` crate module. The whole trigger apparatus (activation, ci_failure, cron ticker, loop_ticker, queue_reconcile, recovery, summary_refresh, watch) is deleted in favor of `lfd` verifying GitHub webhooks and translating them inward as attributed `lf chat` notifications — the mind decides how to react.\n\nLarge amounts of code retire alongside it: the Docker executor and its `bollard`/`tar` dependency tree, the entire Python `api`/`cli`/`client` surface (and the `lfq` tool it powered), the secrets subsystem across Rust/Swift/UI, and the wave scheduler. Observation is now plain tmux rather than a bespoke session API. Docs, demos, and DTO fixtures are rewritten to match.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Wave runtime relocated to `rust/loopflow/src/wave/` — new `resident`, `runtime`, `server`, `supervisor`, `channel`, `wire` modules; one persistent codex thread per wave\n- Store moved from `lfd/store` to `lfdb`; migrations carried over, plus `050_drop_trigger_organs`\n- Trigger subsystem removed; GitHub webhooks (`hooks.rs`) rewritten to emit attributed chat notifications\n- Docker executor deleted (executor collapses to a single wave executor); `bollard`, `tar`, `schemars` and friends dropped from the lockfile\n- Python `api.py`/`cli.py`/`client.py` and the `lfq` tool removed; Rust `lf q` / `lf sub` / `lf op queue` take over dispatch and query\n- Secrets provider removed across Rust, Swift, and the Concerto Connections panel\n- Crons now live in `GOAL.md` frontmatter and fire as system turns; README, docs, and demos updated\n- CI drops the api-smoke and docker-smoke jobs; e2e/regression suites pruned to match",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 13937,
      "deletions": 28258,
      "changed_files": 286
    },
    {
      "number": 802,
      "title": "lf op: plan-first rebase and worktree placement",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op rebase --plan          # show the reset/rebase strategy without touching git\nlf op rebase                 # apply the planned update\nlf op rebase origin/main     # rebase onto an explicit target\n\nlf op wt create my-feature --plan          # print the placement plan only\nlf op wt create child --stack parent       # create parent.child\nlf op wt create my-feature --main          # force a root branch from main\nlf op wt create feature --fork             # independent root from the review base\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nBoth `lf op rebase` and `lf op wt create` now classify the branch and print a plan before mutating git. Rebase distinguishes disposable branches (reset to base), stack children (rebase onto parent), and authored work (normal rebase), stashing `scratch/` under `.lf/scratch-stash/` when a reset would otherwise blow it away and restoring it afterward. Worktree creation is modeled as an explicit placement request — root, main, stack, or fork — that resolves to a strategy (create root, create stack child, checkout existing, reuse worktree) you can inspect with `--plan`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `PlacementRequest`/`PlacementPlan`/`PlacementStrategy` types in `worktrees.rs`; `plan_placement` and `create_from_placement_plan` split planning from execution.\n- `WorktreeSegment`/`StackBranch` newtypes enforce that dots are reserved for stack ancestry — use `--stack` for parent/child, hyphens within a segment.\n- `--plan`, `--main`, `--fork`, and optional-parent `--stack` flags on `lf op wt create`; `--plan` and explicit-target support on `lf op rebase`.\n- Collapsed `RebasePlan.scratch_stash` to a bool and dropped unwired enum variants.\n- Regression test for leading-space porcelain path parsing; e2e test `tests/e2e/test_rebase_efficiency.sh`; updated `docs/lfop.md`, `LOOPFLOW.md`, and goldens.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1207,
      "deletions": 72,
      "changed_files": 21
    },
    {
      "number": 803,
      "title": "compress: drop postgres backend, lf q command, and docker lfd path",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf build \"unify button variants\" --wave designer --dispatch\nlf implement \"Add the endpoint\" --wave engbot --dispatch\n```\n\nWorker dispatch now goes through the flow commands (`lf build/implement/... --wave <name> --dispatch`) instead of the removed `lf q worker run`. Same wave semantics — workers inherit the wave's `GOAL.md` and `MEMORY.md` and report back via PRs.\n\n## Summary\n\nA compression pass that removes three parallel code paths that no longer earn their keep. The Postgres `lfdb` backend is gone (SQLite is the single store), the `lf q` command surface is deleted in favor of flow-native dispatch, and the Docker/compose lfd service path is dropped in favor of direct per-OS service dispatch. Alongside the deletions, the conversation/harness plumbing moves out of `lfd` to the top level so it's no longer coupled to the daemon. Net ~4,300 lines removed.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Drop Postgres backend**: delete `lfdb/postgres.rs` and collapse `lfdb/mod.rs`, `catalog.rs`, `migrations.rs`, and `rows.rs` onto SQLite only. Removes the `deadpool-postgres`/`tokio-postgres`/`postgres-protocol`/`postgres-types` dependency tree (and its transitive `whoami`, `parking_lot`, `md-5`, etc.) from `Cargo.lock`.\n- **Remove `lf q`**: delete `lf/commands/q.rs` (~690 lines) and its wiring; workers now dispatch through the flow commands with `--wave`/`--dispatch`. README loses the `q worker run` examples and the `manual`/`loop` modes table.\n- **Drop docker lfd path**: delete `lfd/service/compose.rs` and fold service management into direct `cfg`-gated macOS/Linux dispatch; slim `scripts/concerto-dev.py` accordingly.\n- **Lift conversation plumbing out of `lfd`**: move `lfd/conversations/{turns,types}` into `chat/`, and the `harness/*` (claude/codex/opencode mappings, testdata, opencode runtime) to a top-level `harness` module. Move `wave_config` from `lfd/http/routes` into `engine`.\n- **Housekeeping**: shrink `lfd/config.rs`, add `wave/subscription.rs`, refresh docs (`lfd.md`, `waves.md`, `wave-authoring.md`, architecture) and goals `wave/goals` MEMORY, and update goldens/DTO fixtures to match.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1532,
      "deletions": 5880,
      "changed_files": 120
    }
  ],
  "previous_release_notes": "# v0.9.12\n\nA maintenance release aimed inward, at the machinery that builds, updates, and ships loopflow. Local development and the native Mac host now share one update command instead of two drifting scripts. The DMG release job can no longer hang the pipeline silently. And release notes — including this one — are now written by interpreting the decision ledger against what actually shipped. Nothing here changes the product surface; it makes that surface easier to keep current and to release.\n\n## One update path for local and native hosts\n\nLocal dev and the native Mac `lfd` host had grown two implementations of the same operation: pull the default branch, rebuild `lf`/`lfd`, install them into a local bin dir. Two copies meant two things to test and a standing risk that the Mac host would drift from the developer path. This cycle collapses them onto one. `scripts/install.py` now owns local installation end to end, and the native host updater calls it directly.\n\n- **`install.py refresh` is the fast CLI-only update** — pulls the default branch (fast-forward only, and it refuses to pull a non-default branch), rebuilds `lf`/`lfd`, installs them atomically. `--no-pull` and `--install-dir` are there when you need them.\n- **`install.py local --use` stays the full build-and-promote path** for `lf`, `lfd`, and `Loopflow.app`.\n- **`deploy/native-lfd-host.sh` updates via `install.py refresh`**, so the Mac host and a developer's laptop run the same code.\n- **`scripts/pull-local-bin.sh` is now a thin wrapper** that delegates to `install.py refresh` — existing callers keep working; new docs point at `install.py`.\n\n## A release pipeline that fails fast and explains itself\n\nTwo changes harden how a release actually gets out the door. The DMG job could hang indefinitely on a stuck subprocess and spin until the workflow-level limit with no signal why; now every external step is bounded and logs are unbuffered, so a stuck step fails fast with a clear message. Separately, the release-note step was rebuilt around the split this document is written to: `DECISIONS.md` carries intent, merged PRs and diffs carry shipped behavior, and the note interprets one against the other.\n\n- **DMG build hangs are bounded** — `build-dmg` gets a 45-minute cap plus tighter per-step limits (35m build/sign/notarize, 30m `notarytool --wait`, 20m cargo/swift, 10m dmg creation, 5m codesign/staple, 5m R2 upload). The release script runs with `python3 -u` and flushed logging, so CI progress is visible as it happens instead of after the fact.\n- **Release notes fuse intent and behavior** — the `release-notes` step treats decisions as the *why* and commits as the *what shipped*, producing an outcome-oriented story (opening → thematic sections → operational notes → small changes). The raw ledger stays archived under `release/v<version>/DECISIONS.md`.\n- **`lf op release notes` and `lf op release run` share one step** — standalone notes, weekly releases, and repo consumers like Cadenza now use the same prompt contract instead of each embedding its own note writer.\n\n## Operational notes\n\n- Deploy scripts and docs that referenced `pull-local-bin.sh` still work through the wrapper, but should migrate to `scripts/install.py refresh`.\n- No config, DTO, or schema changes this cycle; upgrading is a rebuild.\n",
  "decisions": "# Decisions\n\n## 2026-06-30 — A chord is a wave with children; family terms replace \"members\"\n\n**Context:** The `goals` redesign revisited the wave/chord data model. Chords once had their own `chords` + `chord_members` tables (migration 011), which were dropped (migration 028) and folded into the `waves` table via `parent_wave_id`, `wave_type`, and `position`. The structure was already self-referential, but the vocabulary still carried \"member\" from the abandoned table, and the chord concept read as a separate type rather than a kind of wave.\n\n**Decision:** A **chord is just a wave with children** — `wave_type = chord`, children pointing back via `parent_wave_id`. No separate chord entity, no `chord_members`. The relationship vocabulary is **parent / child / sibling**, not \"member.\" The wave-structure domain drops \"member\" entirely. (The VSM/govern \"member\" — `s2-scan` member backlogs, `s5-scan` member configs — is a different domain; rename later only if it bleeds into the wave structure.)\n\n**Implications:** One self-referential `waves` table expresses both waves and chords. Code, docs, and APIs in the wave-structure domain say parent/child/sibling. This simplifies queries (a chord's contents = children where `parent_wave_id = id`) and removes the two-concepts (wave vs chord) split in favor of one type with a `wave_type` discriminator.\n\n## 2026-06-30 — RLM is removed; goals are the subagent-running framework\n\n**Context:** RLM (Recursive Language Model) was a map-reduce framework — split an oversized input into chunks, fan out cheap sub-agents, aggregate — shipped as a ~150-line `RLM.md` injected unconditionally into *every* prompt's system section, plus config knobs (`rlm_agent`, `rlm_max_parallel`, `rlm_max_depth`) and depth-guard env machinery (`RLM_DEPTH` auto-increment, `propagate_rlm_env`, `seed_rlm_env`). The `goals` redesign makes the looping Wave the orchestrator that dispatches flows as inner work — its own, narrower model for running sub-agents. RLM's always-on prompt tax and its capacity-oriented framing no longer earn their place. (Supersedes the 2026-06-24 line \"`RLM` becomes the unconditional system section.\")\n\n**Decision:** Delete RLM entirely — the doc, the const, the `<lf:rlm>` system-section injection, the config fields, and the depth-guard env machinery. The goals operating prompt (the universal \"you are a looping orchestrator, delegate don't implement\" contract carried in the launch system layer) is the new home for subagent-running guidance. The runaway stop-condition becomes goals' own blocks→human, not `RLM_MAX_DEPTH`.\n\n**Implications:** Every prompt drops ~150 lines of always-on map-reduce playbook. The map-reduce *technique* (chunk a huge input) is no longer documented in-prompt; if it's wanted back, it returns as an invokable step (`.lf/steps/`), not an unconditional injection. No recursion depth guard ships until the goals framework provides one — acceptable because the autonomous loop's stop-condition is human escalation by design.\n\n## 2026-06-30 — Goals: a looping prompt primitive that supersedes direction\n\n**Context:** `direction` was removed as machinery on 2026-06-19 (wave model is `area × flow`; direction text redistributed into step-skills and AGENTS.md). The `goals` redesign introduces a persistent **Looping Agent** per wave that runs a **Goal** — a prompt run in a loop — steered by a live Asana roadmap, reusing existing steps/flows as inner work.\n\n**Decision:** Add **goal** as a third prompt primitive alongside step (run once) and flow (steps composed). A goal is \"a step that loops.\" It is the conceptual reincarnation of direction in its *looping, measurable* form, and the product developer's primary authoring surface (\"I just want loopflow to be a good way to run my agents\"). The bar: builtin steps/flows must be expressive enough to build **the clients and the servers** (mobile client, CLI client, server) from goals with **zero step authoring**. Backend priority follows the 2026-06-19 vendor-handoff thesis: codex/claude cloud sessions **first** (adapt to the developer's workflow), hosted lfd + Ghostty **second** (ours to own). Self-extension (goals authoring steps) is the rare, mostly-internal path — no permission cage.\n\n**Implications:** New `goal/` primitive with the standard `.lf/` override model. The loop ticker (cold stateless runs) is replaced by a persistent Looping Agent reading the goal prompt each iteration. `pm.rs` inverts from down-mirror to live Asana read + write-back. Concerto surfaces per-repo looping sessions (launcher+link for cloud, dashboard for hosted lfd). Vocabulary gaps to close: greenfield scaffold, run-the-artifact, client↔server integration, platform-specific build/test. Tracked in `wave/goals/`.\n\n## 2026-06-30 — A wave's authored surface collapses to one `goal.md`; YAMLs, local roadmap, and area retire\n\n**Context:** A wave was configured across three on-disk artifacts: `wave/<name>/<name>.yaml` (the `WaveConfig` seed — flow, goal, mode, area, direction, plus machinery), `wave/<name>/*.md` (a local roadmap mirror), and `.lf/goals/<name>.md` (the goal prompt). That is three files expressing one intent, and it splits the human's authoring surface. Separately, `area` (~300 refs) exists to scope an agent to relevant code — a crutch from when agents flailed without a file pointer. In practice the maintainer does not use it; agents self-scope well enough, and the context that matters (goal, metrics, roadmap) is not a file glob.\n\n**Decision:** The human-authored surface for a wave is **one `goal.md`** — the loop prompt as body, metrics (and remaining intent) as frontmatter. The `wave/<name>/<name>.yaml` config retires; genuine machinery (crons, triggers, workers, pm) either moves to frontmatter or a thin config, decided when `goal.md`'s shape lands. The **roadmap moves to Asana** — the local `wave/<name>/*.md` mirror is retired and the goal agent reads (and writes) Asana directly. **`area` is deprecated**: left dormant, not removed in a rush, and nothing new is built on it; a dedicated later PR strips the plumbing.\n\n**Implications:** One file to author, read, and diff per wave. `goal.md` becomes the contract the goal-authoring agent and the Concerto launch button both depend on, so its shape is sequenced first. Metrics enter the model for the first time (open fork: prose success-criteria the agent judges, vs. structured fields the loop measures). Waves without an Asana backlog lose their roadmap source — acceptable, since the redesign targets Asana-backed waves. `area` stops being a first-class wave dimension; existing plumbing is inert until removed.\n\n## 2026-06-30 — The looping agent captures to the roadmap, then dispatches — it never solves inline\n\n**Context:** The goals operating prompt told the Looping Agent to \"delegate, don't implement,\" but left the mechanics vague (\"when interactive subagent sessions are available\"). The maintainer wants a stronger, concrete discipline: the agent must not directly solve a subproject it identifies, and every unit of work must stay externally visible and steerable — not buried in the orchestrator's own transcript.\n\n**Decision:** The operating contract is **capture, then dispatch**. When the goal agent picks a substantial next move, it first writes it to the roadmap as an **Asana task**, then launches a flow **against that task** (`lf <flow>: <task>`), which runs as a **steerable subagent session** the human can monitor and redirect. Only atomic fixes dispatch directly. Fan-out is a gated escalation: when budget allows and the roadmap holds well-scoped independent tasks, launch **parallel** subagents (one flow per task); spawn a **child wave** when a task is itself a looping unit. The operating prompt states the full contract from turn one even though parts (budget primitive, Asana write-back, Concerto monitor/steer) are not yet wired — deliberately, so the prompt pulls the runtime forward rather than trailing it.\n\n**Implications:** The roadmap (Asana) becomes the single source of truth for what is being worked — no work exists that isn't a task. Every subagent is observable and interruptible by construction. Four runtime capabilities are now owed by the prompt: a budget primitive (`wave/goals/2-wave-budget.md`), Asana write-back (`2-asana-roadmap.md`), flow-bound-to-Asana-task dispatch, and Concerto monitor/steer of looping sessions (`3-concerto-looping-sessions.md`). Until they land, the corresponding prompt clauses are aspirational.\n\n## 2026-06-30 — Repo holds authored intent; lfd holds runtime state. A Wave *is* a goal agent.\n\n**Context:** Designing the `goal.md` collapse surfaced a boundary the old `wave/<name>/<name>.yaml` violated: it seeded lfd runtime wiring (crons, triggers) *from the repo*, fusing two things that belong at different layers. It also framed a wave as a config row that *has* a goal, rather than as the looping agent itself.\n\n**Decision:** A **Wave is a goal agent** — the looping orchestrator itself, not a container that holds a goal loop. The authored surface is one file, `wave/<name>/goal.md` (frontmatter = intent: prompt, `metrics`, `roadmap`, `agent`, `workers`, `primary_flow`; body = the loop prompt). **Repo = authored intent; lfd = runtime state.** Crons and triggers are a *lower* primitive than the goal agent — flow-level reflexes (\"on signal X, fire flow Y\"), no agent reasoning in the loop. They have two origins: **authored** reflexes a human records in `.lf/crons`/`.lf/triggers` to make them portable/installable (deliberate, like `.lf/steps`), and **wave-spawned** reflexes the goal agent creates while operating (lfd-only state, never written to disk). Neither lives in `goal.md`. The runtime does not auto-dump every reflex a wave creates to the repo.\n\n**Implications:** Killing the wave YAML separates repo-authored intent from lfd-managed runtime, rather than just merging files. Wave creation sources only intent fields from `goal.md`; crons/triggers lose their repo seed path and become lfd-only (with an optional, deliberate `.lf/crons`/`.lf/triggers` authoring path for portability, a later milestone). Metrics enter as a frontmatter list of prose criteria the loop self-judges — no measurement engine until a specific loop needs a hard exit. Build order: **M1** `goal.md` as the authored surface (retire the YAML incl. its crons/triggers seed path) *plus the ruthless collapse below*; **M2** live Asana `roadmap:` read + write-back; **M3** `.lf/crons`/`.lf/triggers` portable reflex definitions; later Concerto author/spawn/steer and the budget primitive.\n\n## 2026-06-30 — Ruthless collapse: keep the loop handles, cut the old `area × flow × direction` model\n\n**Context:** Reflecting on the cumulative goal-primitive changes, the wave model still encoded a superseded design — a wave as `area × flow × direction`, woken by a cold ticker, configured by a repo YAML. We want the model focused on what a wave is *now*: a goal agent. A usage map (read-only) sized each candidate and caught two traps a blind delete would have hit.\n\n**Decision:** Keep only the **loop handles** the operating contract names — *flow* (`primary_flow`, first-class on the record), *roadmap* (Asana), *metrics* — plus the *goal* prompt. Cut, as part of M1: `area` (its context-scoping use), wave-level `direction` (distinct from and not touching the `.lf/directions/` doc system), `step_agents`, `serialized`, the `WaveConfig` all-`Optional`/`Default` YAML-mirror shape, and the serde-enforcement tests (`*_is_required` — they test serde, not the product; the type already carries the requirement). Collapse the `Wave`/`WaveConfig` duplication: `WaveConfig` = the parsed `goal.md` (required fields), `Wave` = a running instance wrapping it, no duplicated config fields. **Two corrections from the map:** (1) `WaveMode::Manual` is *not* cut — it is load-bearing (`list_loopable_waves` filters `mode='loop'`; Manual exempts cron + inline/goal waves from the ticker and gates branch advancement); it is an old-activation-model artifact that dies with the activation redesign (M2+), not now. (2) `area` scoping does *not* simply vanish: repo-trigger path scoping moves onto the trigger itself (an explicit path-pattern field — a reflex should own what it watches), and `ops/release.rs`'s `config.area` is a *different* concept (release-target manifest/PR scope) that stays.\n\n**Implications:** The wave model shrinks to goal + handles + runtime state. `area` as a wave dimension is deleted (not dormant); the area-gated wave summary is dropped; trigger scoping becomes explicit per-trigger patterns. Test philosophy sharpens: stop writing tests that assert the type system's own guarantees. The activation model (ticker, `mode`, crons/triggers) is explicitly *not* redesigned in M1 — that's M2+, and `mode`/Manual survives until then.\n\n## 2026-07-02 — lfd runtime ontology is Wave, Run, Session\n\n**Context:** The goal-agent runtime cleanup exposed four overlapping lifecycle nouns: `WaveRun` for Wave-scoped execution and PR lineage, `AgentRun` for backend process state, `TerminalSession` for tmux/Ghostty attachability, and `Session` for transcript/input/usage events. Launch APIs added a fifth noun, `AgentLaunch`, as an envelope around the thing callers actually needed. This duplicated Codex/opencode's simpler shape, where \"session\" is the live conversation/control surface and \"agent\" is the performer or mode, not a durable record.\n\n**Decision:** The lfd product model has three core nouns: **Wave**, **Run**, and **Session**. `Wave` is durable goal/memory/orchestration identity. `Run` is one agent invocation's execution/result lineage and absorbs old `WaveRun` + `AgentRun`. `Session` is the attachable live conversation/control surface and absorbs old `Session` + `TerminalSession`. Launching a Wave agent or worker returns a `Session`; `AgentLaunch` is deleted.\n\n**Implications:** Public/core Rust types, HTTP DTOs/routes, Python/lfq models, Swift/Concerto models, and fixture names move to `Run`, `Session`, `run_id`, and `session_id`. `/v0/wave_runs` and `/v0/terminal-sessions` collapse into `/v0/runs` and `/v0/sessions`. Transport-specific UI code may still say tmux, Ghostty, or terminal when it is actually dealing with that transport; product/API code does not. Backwards compatibility aliases are not preserved for this branch.\n"
}